Internal energy change ΔE = E₂ − E₁ is the difference between the internal energies of the final and initial states of the system; by the first law, ΔE = q + w. Enthalpy change ΔH = H₂ − H₁ is the corresponding difference in enthalpy, H = E + PV; at constant pressure ΔH = ΔE + PΔV = q_p.
ΔE and ΔH are equal when PΔV = 0, that is:
(i) at constant volume, where ΔV = 0 and no pressure–volume work is done;
(ii) when there is no change in the number of moles of gas, Δn_g = 0, so PΔV = Δn_gRT = 0;
(iii) for reactions involving only solids and liquids, where the volume change is so small that ΔH ≈ ΔE.
Example: H₂(g) + Cl₂(g) → 2HCl(g) has Δn_g = 0, so ΔH = ΔE. For 2H₂(g) + O₂(g) → 2H₂O(l), Δn_g = −3, so ΔH = ΔE − 3RT and the two quantities are not equal.
